.TH GLADOS 1 "July 2026" "Quant 1.0.0" "Quant Language Tools"
.SH NAME
glados \- Quant language compiler, runner, and project manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glados compiler
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B glados
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glados
is the main entry point for the Quant language toolchain. It acts as
both a direct compiler/runner for single files and a project manager
(like \fBcargo\fR or \fBcabal\fR) for projects organised around a
\fBquant.toml\fR manifest.
.PP
Quant is a statically-typed, C-like scripting language with a stack-based
bytecode VM, generics, structs, error handling, and a full FFI for calling
C libraries via \fIlibffi\fR.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS Direct compilation
.TP
.B compiler \fIFILE\fR
Lex, parse, type-check, compile, and execute \fIFILE\fR. Accepts
\fB\-\-stdlib\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR, \fB\-\-load\fR, \fB\-\-dump\fR,
\fB\-\-native\fR, and \fB\-\-emit\-c\fR (see OPTIONS below).
.SS Project management
The following subcommands operate on a project in the current directory.
A project requires a \fBquant.toml\fR manifest. Example manifest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[project]
name    = "my-project"
version = "0.1.0"
entry   = "src/main.qa"
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B init \fINAME\fR
Scaffold a new project in a directory named \fINAME\fR. Creates
\fBquant.toml\fR, \fBsrc/main.qa\fR (hello-world template), and
\fBtests/\fR.
.TP
.B build [\-\-release] [\-\-target \fITARGET\fR]
Compile the project entry point. With \fB\-\-release\fR, enable
optimisations. Bytecode is written to \fI.build/\fR. With
\fB\-\-target c\fR, translate the compiled bytecode to C and produce a
native binary at \fI.build/main\fR (the generated C is kept at
\fI.build/main.c\fR); requires a C compiler and the Quant C runtime
(see ENVIRONMENT).
.TP
.B run [\fIARGS\fR]
Compile and immediately execute the project, forwarding any extra
arguments to the program.
.TP
.B test [\-\-cov] [\-\-cov\-min \fIPCT\fR] [\-\-cov\-out \fIFILE\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
Discover and run all test functions. Test files must match
\fI*_test.qa\fR inside the \fBtests/\fR directory (configurable in
\fBquant.toml\fR). Any function whose name begins with \fBtest_\fR and
takes no parameters is treated as a test case.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-cov
Print a full coverage report after the test run showing three metrics:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfn\fR \(em function coverage: which functions in \fIsrc/\fR were called.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBln\fR \(em line coverage: which executable source lines were reached,
tracked via \fBCOV_MARK\fR bytecode probes emitted at every statement.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbr\fR \(em branch coverage: for every \fBif\fR/\fBwhile\fR condition,
whether both the true and false outcomes were observed during the test run,
tracked via \fBCOV_BRANCH\fR probes inserted after each condition expression.
.RE
Per-file and overall totals are displayed with progress bars.
Covered functions are shown with \fB+\fR, uncovered with \fB\-\fR.
Files listed in \fBquant.toml\fR \fB[test] cov_ignore\fR are excluded.
.TP
.B \-\-cov\-min \fIPCT\fR
Fail with exit status 1 if total function coverage is below \fIPCT\fR
percent. Implies \fB\-\-cov\fR output. Use in CI to enforce a minimum
coverage threshold.
.TP
.B \-\-cov\-out \fIFILE\fR
Write the coverage report as a JSON file to \fIFILE\fR. The JSON contains
per-file and per-function covered/total/pct fields. Implies \fB\-\-cov\fR
output.
.RE
.br
Exits with status 1 if any test fails or if \fB\-\-cov\-min\fR is set and
the threshold is not met.
.TP
.B bench [\fIFILE\fR]
Discover and run microbenchmarks. Without \fIFILE\fR, scans
\fBbenchmarks/\fR and the test directory for \fI*_bench.qa\fR files;
with \fIFILE\fR, runs only that file. Each parameterless function
whose name begins with \fBbench_\fR is executed; such functions call
\fBbench_fn\fR (from the \fBbench\fR standard-library module), which
times a closure across several samples and prints its throughput
(\fBns/op\fR) and standard deviation in a TAP-compatible line.
.TP
.B native\-diff [\fIPATH\fR...]
Differential test harness for the native backend. Runs every
\fI.qa\fR file found under the given paths (default: \fBtests/\fR)
under both the bytecode VM and a natively compiled binary, then
diffs stdout and exit codes. Files the C translator does not cover
yet are reported as skips (the translator rejects unsupported
instructions and builtins at compile time, so a skip can never hide
a semantics difference). Native binaries are written to
\fI.build/native\-diff/\fR. Exits with status 1 if any file produces
different output under the two engines. Requires a C compiler and
the Quant C runtime (see ENVIRONMENT).
.TP
.B lint
Run
.BR wheatley (1)
over all source files in the project.
.TP
.B watch [\fICMD\fR...]
Watch the project's \fI.qa\fR source files (under \fIsrc/\fR and the
test directory) and re-run a subcommand whenever one changes. \fICMD\fR
is the subcommand to run, defaulting to \fBbuild\fR; any flags are
forwarded to it (for example \fBglados watch test \-\-cov\fR). The
command runs once at startup and then after every change; file
modification times are polled roughly every 400 ms. Runs until
interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B fmt [\-\-check]
Run
.BR quant\-fmt (1)
over all source files. With \fB\-\-check\fR, exit 1 if any file
would be changed (useful in CI).
.TP
.B doc [\-\-format \fIFMT\fR] [\-\-out \fIDIR\fR]
Generate API documentation from doc comments (\fI//\fR above public
functions). \fIFMT\fR is \fBhtml\fR (default) or \fBmd\fR. Output
goes to \fIdocs/api/\fR by default.
.TP
.B clean
Remove build artefacts (\fI.build/\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
The following options apply to the \fBcompiler\fR subcommand:
.TP
.B \-\-stdlib \fIDIR\fR
Override the standard library search path. By default glados looks for
stdlib modules in (in order): the \fBQUANT_STDLIB\fR environment variable,
\fI/usr/local/share/quant/lib\fR, then \fI./std\fR as a development fallback.
.TP
.B \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Write compiled bytecode to \fIFILE\fR instead of executing it. The bytecode
can later be loaded with \fB\-\-load\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-load \fIFILE\fR
Execute a previously compiled bytecode file, skipping compilation entirely.
.TP
.B \-\-dump
Print disassembled bytecode to stdout instead of executing. Useful for
debugging the compiler or understanding codegen output.
.TP
.B \-\-native \fIBIN\fR
Compile to a native binary at \fIBIN\fR: the bytecode is translated to
C, compiled with the system C compiler, and linked against the Quant C
runtime. The generated C is kept at \fIBIN\fR\fB.c\fR for inspection.
The native backend currently covers a subset of the language
(functions, control flow, arithmetic, arrays, core printing builtins);
unsupported programs fail at compile time with the offending
instruction.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-c \fIFILE\fR
Write the generated C translation unit to \fIFILE\fR without invoking
the C compiler.
.SH QUANT.TOML FIELDS
.TP
.B [project] name
Project name (string, required).
.TP
.B [project] version
Semantic version string (required).
.TP
.B [project] entry
Relative path to the main source file (default: \fBsrc/main.qa\fR).
.TP
.B [project] stdlib
Override stdlib path for this project (optional).
.TP
.B [test] dir
Directory to search for test files (default: \fBtests/\fR).
.TP
.B [test] cov_ignore
Inline TOML array of file paths (relative to the project root) to exclude
from the \fBglados test \-\-cov\fR coverage report.
Patterns are matched as path suffixes, so \fB"main.qa"\fR matches any file
ending in \fBmain.qa\fR.
.br
Example: \fBcov_ignore = ["src/main.qa", "src/generated.qa"]\fR
.TP
.B [doc] out
Output directory for generated documentation (default: \fBdocs/api/\fR).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QUANT_STDLIB
Absolute path to the standard library directory. Overrides the compiled-in
default and is itself overridden by \fB\-\-stdlib\fR.
.TP
.B QUANT_RUNTIME_DIR
Directory containing the C runtime sources (\fBquant_runtime.c\fR /
\fBquant_runtime.h\fR) used by \fB\-\-native\fR and
\fBbuild \-\-target c\fR. Defaults to \fI./runtime\fR then
\fI/usr/local/share/quant/runtime\fR.
.TP
.B QUANT_CC
C compiler command used by the native backend (default: \fBcc\fR).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful compilation, execution, or command.
.TP
.B 1
Compilation error (lex, parse, type, or codegen), runtime error, test
failure, or missing manifest.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a Quant program directly:
.PP
.RS
.B glados compiler hello.qa
.RE
.PP
Create and run a new project:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glados init hello-world
cd hello-world
glados run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile to bytecode and run later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glados compiler \-\-output hello.qbc hello.qa
glados compiler \-\-load hello.qbc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check formatting and lint in CI:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glados fmt \-\-check
glados lint
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile to a native binary and cross-check it against the VM:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glados compiler \-\-native hello hello.qa
glados native\-diff tests/
.fi
.RE
